00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:03.210
STEPHANIE WONG: Regional.

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.220
Nearline.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:06.442
Coldline.

00:00:06.442 --> 00:00:07.650
IVAN RAMIREZ: Hey, Stephanie.

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:08.740
What are you doing?

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:09.420
STEPHANIE WONG: I'm
trying to organize

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.460
my objects into the
appropriate storage classes.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:12.377
IVAN RAMIREZ: But why?

00:00:12.377 --> 00:00:14.543
STEPHANIE WONG: Because if
I organize them properly,

00:00:14.543 --> 00:00:15.860
I could save a lot of money.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:16.710
IVAN RAMIREZ: Well, that I know.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.300
But why are you doing
them one by one?

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.550
Didn't you know you could
automate this entire process

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:22.800
simply by using lifecycle
management rules?

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.040
STEPHANIE WONG: Sounds like
you're about to show me how.

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:25.456
IVAN RAMIREZ: Let's
jump right in.

00:00:25.456 --> 00:00:27.558
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:31.422 --> 00:00:33.500
If we're talking about
lifecycle management,

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.050
we need to have a
clear understanding

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:36.550
of the different
prices and features

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:38.680
that each one of the
different classes provides.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.930
So let's go ahead and go through
a high-level overview of them.

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:43.630
First, we have
multi-regional storage,

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:45.269
which will provide
geo-redundancy,

00:00:45.269 --> 00:00:47.560
meaning that it's generally
good for storing frequently

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.480
and globally accessed content.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:50.980
Then we have regional
storage, which

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:53.597
is great for data needing to
be kept in a single region,

00:00:53.597 --> 00:00:55.180
meaning that it's
best suited for data

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:58.540
sets that will only be accessed
from a particular region.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:00.320
Then we also have
nearline storage,

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.950
which is meant for
infrequently accessed data.

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:05.590
Here you pay a cheaper
price for storing your data.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:08.020
But you must also pay
for retrieving it.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:10.270
The math works out so that
if you access the data less

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:12.040
than once per month,
then you are actually

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.290
saving money by using nearline.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:16.180
And lastly, we have coldline.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:18.500
The SLAs for coldline
are the same as nearline.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.730
But the price for storing
an object is even lower.

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:23.890
Here we also have a fee
for retrieving the data.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:25.390
And in this case,
the math works out

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:27.700
so that if you access the
data less than once per year,

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.260
you'll be saving money.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.510
Now, lifecycle management
is an easy and simple way

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:33.730
to automatically move
objects from one storage

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:36.370
class to another by simply
setting a set of rules

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:38.560
instead of having to do
the process manually.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:41.260
The idea is that if you
are not accessing objects

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:42.940
within specific
time periods, there

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:44.819
is a substantial amount
of money that you

00:01:44.819 --> 00:01:47.110
could save by taking advantage
of the different storage

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:48.280
classes.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.020
The different rules
by which you could

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.530
set these automatic
policies are the following.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:57.220
Age, Created Before, IsLive,
which is basically just asking

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:00.220
if you have the latest
version, and whether it matches

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:01.711
a specific storage class.

00:02:01.711 --> 00:02:03.460
Let's go into the
console now and show you

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:06.037
how easy this is to set up.

00:02:06.037 --> 00:02:07.620
So now that we're
in the console we're

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:08.995
going to click on
the burger menu

00:02:08.995 --> 00:02:11.550
and go to the storage
section of the console.

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:14.519
Here you'll see a bucket that I
created called Take Five Demos.

00:02:14.519 --> 00:02:16.310
And you'll see that no
lifecycle management

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:17.640
rules have been specified.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.015
If we go into the bucket, you
can see a series of objects

00:02:20.015 --> 00:02:22.860
that I added and two different
dates about three days apart

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:27.440
from each other, on the 18th
and on the 25th of this month.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:29.200
Now, we'll go back
to the main page

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:32.930
and actually add some of these
lifecycle management rules.

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:34.640
And click on None.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.820
And we'll go ahead
and add a rule.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:39.300
Here we can specify the type
of rule that we want to add--

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:42.760
Age, Created By, and so on
that we described earlier.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:45.540
We'll go ahead and select
Age, and we'll define the age.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.040
In this case, we'll
set it to three.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:53.140
We'll go ahead and continue,
and say what this rule will do,

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:55.750
which in this case we'll
set the objects to coldline.

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:57.646
We'll go ahead and
save this rule.

00:02:57.646 --> 00:02:58.685
That's another example.

00:02:58.685 --> 00:03:01.150
We'll go ahead and
add another rule.

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:03.863
This time if any object
is older than one day

00:03:03.863 --> 00:03:06.960
it will automatically set
that object to nearline.

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:15.480
Now that we've
setup these rules,

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.880
let's go back to our
objects within our bucket

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:19.999
to see how they've
been classified.

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:21.790
Now, we sped up this
process for the video,

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:24.030
but this usually
takes about a day.

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:26.040
Now you can see all of
our different objects

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:27.400
within the bucket.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.175
And you can see that
the storage class

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:31.620
has changed to coldline
for some of them

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.780
and regionals for others.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.310
Now, this was a
really quick demo

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:37.170
of how you can use
lifecycle management

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:38.310
to manage your objects.

